  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Generalized aching and marked fatigue for ~6 weeks.\n- History of present illness / Symptoms: Gradual onset ~end of January–early February (patient estimates symptoms began a couple weeks after returning from a holiday in the USA). Symptoms are generalized muscle/joint aches (shoulders, back, hips, knees), persistent tiredness with reduced daytime energy and concentration, reduced appetite (smaller portions) but no reported weight change. A single cutaneous lesion on posterior calf noted in mid–late February that faded over a couple of weeks (patient has a photo). Patient recalled a mild sore throat and possible low-grade fever around the time of the rash. No cough, no shortness of breath, no GI symptoms, no urinary symptoms, no focal neurological symptoms (no headaches beyond generalized, no visual changes, no paresthesia). Able to continue working but less effective; reduced exercise tolerance.\n- Past medical history: Asthma, hay fever.\n- Medications / Allergies: Uses a daily preventive inhaler (morning and night). Takes an over-the-counter antihistamine. No drug allergies documented in the transcript.\n- Family / Social history: Lives with wife. Works in health technology. Recent travel to the United States (end Jan/early Feb) with a lot of walking; wore shorts and boots (possible environmental/tick exposure). Drinks alcohol occasionally. Smoking status not clearly stated (patient did not answer when asked). No mention of relevant family history.\n- Vital signs / exam findings: None documented in transcript (no vitals recorded). Rash described only by patient as photo on posterior calf which faded; no current rash on exam.\n- Labs / imaging: None performed yet. Doctor recommended baseline bloods (CBC, renal/liver) and specific testing including Lyme serology given history and rash.",
